34,2,,status,Jonathan,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"My findings are concerning. If we invest in something requiring external appraisals, our quarter-end will become an absolute nightmare of delays and costs.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"I've found that our capacity to help with staffing is very lopsided. We can find executives, but the operational boots on the ground are a different story.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"I found some interesting things. It seems that when we charge more, some customers stay quite happily, while others leave us very quickly.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"The situation is precarious. Our credit line is useless for software-style contracts. If we invest in a high-burn model, we have no way to borrow against it.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Shion,,"It seems the ""unforeseen"" is actually predictable. If the product touches sensitive data or hardware, the deployment delay is almost guaranteed.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Misaki, that aligns with my fear. If we can't verify the numbers independently and we can't borrow against them, we are flying blind.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shion, if the deployment is delayed, does that mean the hiring needs are pushed back, or just piled up for later?","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Shizue,,I noticed that the companies with physical presence in the customer's environment had much better retention. There is a warmth in that connection.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Shizue, warmth doesn't pay the bills. If we choose a hardware-heavy company, the follow-on calls will be aggressive and frequent.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, it's the latter. The hiring happens, the money burns, but the product sits in a security queue. It's a waste of capital.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,I cannot support a candidate that forces us to rely on third-party appraisals. I want a holding where we can check the figures ourselves.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,I am wary of any candidate that requires a massive influx of field staff. Our recruiters simply cannot deliver that quickly.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"I feel we should favor the one that has a deep, lasting bond with its users. A business that survives a price hike is a healthy one.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Misaki,,We must prioritize a candidate with low follow-on requirements. We cannot afford another quarter of depleting our reserves without a way to leverage.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Shion,,"I will vote against anything that requires a security review of a legacy system. I'm not interested in waiting nine months for a ""go-live.""","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
